# CLI surface

**Status: provisional.** Revises §4 of the superseded design document, which was
written when `update` was the only intent. Companion documents: `intents.md`
(what can be asked for) and `reading.md` (how Portfiles are read and edited). Sections marked *(field)* were
changed or added in response to `field-evidence.md`.

---

## What carries over unchanged

Two decisions from the earlier surface are worth keeping verbatim.

**Borrow `port`'s selector grammar wholesale.** `maintainer:me`,
`category:python`, `depof:openssl`, and a bare list of port names, which is
simply a selector that happens to enumerate. A bare invocation resolves to
`maintainer:me`. One resolver, one targeting vocabulary, identical across every
verb and identical to the tool already in the user's fingers. Do not invent
`--mine` / `--all` / `--category`; that is how a second vocabulary is born.

**The plan artifact is the human checkpoint.** A y/n prompt inside a flow works
locally and is structurally unusable in CI. A serialized plan that one command
emits and another consumes gives dry-run, reviewable diffs, a testable unit
smaller than "the whole command", and a CI story that falls out rather than
requiring a parallel path.

## The axis collision

The earlier surface has verbs `status`, `plan`, `apply`, `promote`, `env` — and
every example invokes `dockhand update <selector>`, which is not among them.
That is not an oversight; it is two axes competing for one slot. `update` is an
**intent**; `plan` is a **pipeline stage**. With one intent in the design both
fit in the verb position without visible conflict. With eleven they do not.

**The intent takes the verb slot, and `plan` becomes the implicit default
output of every intent verb.**

```
dockhand bump gcc14 --to 1.4.2           # emits a plan; changes nothing
dockhand bump maintainer:me --to latest  # a sweep of bumps; still only plans
dockhand apply <plan>                    # materialise into the worktree
dockhand verify <plan> --depth=build
dockhand promote <plan>                  # branch, push, PR
```

This preserves the earlier surface's best safety property — the convenient
default and the safe default are the same default — while letting the intent be
the thing actually typed. It also matches `port`'s own shape (`port upgrade`,
`port uninstall`), which is the same fingers-already-know argument that
justified borrowing the selectors.

For single-port interactive use, three commands is tedious, so `apply` reads a
plan on stdin:

```
dockhand bump gcc14 --to 1.4.2 | dockhand apply -
```

The pipe is there when wanted and absent from sweeps, where it never is.

### Intent verbs

```
bump <sel> [--to <version>|latest]
revbump <sel>
epoch <sel>
refresh-checksums <sel>            # never auto-promotable; carries its cause
vendor <sel>                       # regenerate vendored block (T3)
deps <sel> --add/--remove <spec> [--kind lib|build|run] [--variant <v>]
patches <sel> [--drop-obsolete]
modify <sel> --set <field>=<value> # scalar, no-cascade fields only
obsolete <sel> --replaced-by <port>
migrate <sel> --idiom <name>
```

**`refresh-checksums`, not `checksums`.** `port checksum` already exists as a
phase, and it *verifies* rather than refreshes. Borrowing `port`'s vocabulary to
mean something different is worse than not borrowing it, so the bare noun is a
trap and the verbose name is the honest one.

**`modify` is bounded by rule.** A field is admissible only if setting it has no
cascade and no semantics dockhand would have to own — see the `modify` boundary
in `intents.md`. `version`, `revision`, `checksums`, `depends_*`, `patchfiles`
and `variants` are all excluded, each for a different reason. The name is
unsettled: `modify` promises a generality the rule withholds, and `set` is more
honest about the limit.

**`obsolete` takes a port name, not a file.** `libftdi` obsoletes its top-level
port while two subports stay live, so the selector resolves to ports and the
edit is frequently insertion of a guarded block. It also cascades into `revbump`
and sometimes `epoch`.

`--variant` appears on `deps` and not on `bump`: that is the variant-scope open
question surfacing as a flag, since the intents touching variant-relative
metadata need the scope and the others do not.

### `--to latest` is a query with a confidence, not a value *(field)*

This is the flagship invocation of the flagship intent, and the update signal
underneath it is unreliable in both directions. Across ~50 ports, Repology
reported updates that did not exist for sixteen, under-reported for three, and
for `zot` returned a version belonging to an entirely different project.

So `latest` cannot be sugar for a literal. It is a resolution step that can
fail, disagree across sources, or return the wrong project — which means it
emits findings of its own before any Portfile is read. On a 340-port sweep,
where `plan` is the expensive stage, an unreliable resolver is also the
difference between one expensive pass and a third of one wasted.

---

## The `apply` / `promote` boundary is drawn in the wrong place

The earlier surface splits them on the network — "`apply` never touches the
network. Local, filesystem only." Good instinct about blast radius, wrong line.

A bump's checksums cannot be computed without fetching the distfile. A T3
vendored block cannot be generated without `cargo2port` reaching crates.io or
`go2port` reaching the module proxy. If those run at apply time, `apply` is a
network operation for the flagship intent and for every T3 port.

Move the fetch rather than redrawing the label. **`plan` is the stage that reads
the world.** It resolves `latest`, fetches distfiles, computes checksums, and
invokes the generators. `apply` mutates only the working tree.

The invariant that survives is neither read/write nor local/network — fork-CI
verification broke both, because `verify --where=fork-ci` must *push a branch*
before `promote` runs, and the original table had no row for `verify` at all.
The resource the instinct was always guarding is **other people's attention**:
pushing to your own fork notifies nobody and obligates nobody; opening a PR
pings maintainers and creates a review obligation. Stated as rings of
consequence:

| Ring | Contains | Written by |
|---|---|---|
| 0 | the plan, immutable once emitted | `plan` |
| 1 | the worktree | `apply` |
| 2 | things the user owns and can delete: ephemeral prefixes, sandbox, VM images, fork branches, local caches, dismissal state | `plan` (caches), `verify`, `debug`, `dismiss` |
| 3 | other people's attention: the upstream PR, notifications, review obligations | `promote` — **only** `promote` |

Each stage writes at most its ring. This is "never outrun review capacity"
appearing as a CLI invariant, and it repairs two older wobbles in passing:
`plan` "writes nothing" was always approximate (it populates distcaches —
ring-2 state under D8), and dismissal state has a named home instead of an
unacknowledged D8 violation.

**A branch changes rings.** `verify` force-pushes its branch freely — re-runs
are `push -f`, and `cancel-in-progress` keeps them cheap — *until* `promote`
turns that branch into a PR head. From that moment the branch is ring 3, and
force-pushing it rewrites an open PR under reviewers' feet. The rule with
teeth: **`verify` never pushes to a branch that backs an open PR; after
`promote`, every update to that branch goes through `promote`**, because
updating it spends attention again.

Three settled consequences. *Branch naming* follows the human convention
already observed on a real fork — `<port>-<version>`, not a `dockhand/`
prefix; the ref becomes the PR head, and provenance candour belongs in the PR
body (D9), not the ref name. *Branch accumulation* is prunable without
violating "what is deliberately absent": staleness is derivable (the PR merged
or closed), so sweeping ring-2 garbage needs no state. *Async shape*:
`verify --where=fork-ci` waits with progress by default; `--no-wait` submits
and returns, results arriving as findings via `status --source=fork-ci` — the
findings model, not a job system.

*(field)* Fifty ports of ordinary maintenance produced no case wanting to
violate the ring model; the one apparent violation (fork pushes) is what
forced it to be stated correctly.

### It also resolves plan partiality

The earlier surface left open whether a plan is "a diff plus metadata" or
"metadata embedding a diff", noting that `apply` must reject a plan whose base
Portfile moved underneath it. The cascade makes this worse: above T2 a plan is
partial by construction, because no diff exists until a generator has run.

Putting generator invocation inside `plan` dissolves the problem — by the time a
plan exists, the generator has run and the diff is complete. That is the second
argument for locating the reads there, and the stronger one. The cost is that
`plan` becomes the expensive stage, which is correct: it is the one whose output
is kept.

---

## Findings

```
dockhand status <sel> [--source=local|ci|all]
dockhand dismiss <finding-id> [--reason ...]
dockhand debug <finding-id>            # shell into the preserved environment
dockhand bump --from-finding <finding-id>
```

`status` is the reflex command and deserves naming early even when it reports
one kind of finding.

**A name collision worth avoiding.** The design calls the third finding
disposition "promote to intent", but `promote` already means "open the PR" —
and it is the only verb that touches someone else's repository. Two promotions
in one CLI, one of them that one, is an overload with real consequences.
Expressing it as `--from-finding` on the intent verb keeps the word free and
makes the proposal-never-execution rule structural: a finding seeds a plan, and
a plan still has to be applied and promoted deliberately.

*(field)* `--source` exists because findings demonstrably originate off the
local machine. Three ports built locally and failed on the buildbot, discovered
after merge. "CI is the authority" already implied this; the verb list had
nowhere to put it.

*(field)* A CI finding is not attributable without a baseline. `orbiton`
appeared to have regressed from a change made that session; the failures turned
out to date from January, and separating "my change broke this" from "this was
already broken" required per-builder history. `--source=ci` does not supply
that, and without it every pre-existing red builder is charged to the most
recent change.

*(field)* A finding is not always about the port under intent — `copilot` failed
because its build dependency `packr` had been independently broken for months.
`status` must therefore distinguish *the port under intent* from *the port the
finding is about*, and they are different fields.

---

## Read-only commands

```
dockhand doctor
dockhand classify <sel> [--for <intent>] [--to <version>|latest]
```

`doctor` prints exactly the report D11 describes — *T0–T2 available; T3
unavailable, no `cargo2port`; pristine verification unavailable, no `tart`* —
and the same probe runs implicitly at plan time so a batch refuses before it
starts rather than forty minutes in. Its probe list has grown with the design:
tools (`port-tclsh`, `git` and its version floor, generators, `tart`, `gh`),
the fork (exists, Actions enabled — readable from the runs API), the verdict
prefix template (present, base version, staleness), and the pinned
`(base, mpbb)` pair.

### `classify` is two questions, and they have different costs

The one-pager's claim that classification "costs one evaluation" — and is
therefore cheap enough to sweep the whole tree — holds for only one of them:

- **Static tractability.** *Can dockhand edit this Portfile's version at all?*
  One evaluation, no network, genuinely sweepable. This is what validates the
  recognizer set against 20,033 real Portfiles, and it needs no `--to`.
- **Bump feasibility.** *Will this particular bump work?* Needs the target
  version, needs a livecheck, needs the upstream tree read at the target tag.
  Per-port expensive, and not sweepable in the same sense.

`skopeo` is statically tractable and infeasible. Both answers are correct
answers to different questions, and giving one command both jobs — as the
`--for`/`--to` flags below do — conflates them. They may want separate verbs.

`classify` is the tractability survey: one evaluation per port, across any
selector up to the whole tree. It has no counterpart in the earlier surface and
is plausibly the most valuable early command, because it is how the recognizer
set gets validated against 20,033 real Portfiles and how the empirical tier
distribution — currently guessed at everywhere — is actually obtained.

*(field)* `classify` takes `--for` and `--to` because tractability is not a
property of the Portfile alone. A `skopeo` 1.22→1.24 bump is one literal on one
line, and it was wrong: the Go module path had been renamed upstream. Deciding
that requires reading the target tree, not the Portfile. A classification
without a target version is answering an easier question than the one asked.

*(field)* The structural checks that predicted real breakage across fifty
updates were all of this shape — module path at current tag versus target tag,
location of `package main`, presence of the build driver the Portfile invokes,
`patch --dry-run` per patchfile, whether `-X` ldflag targets still exist as
package-level variables, whether `vendor/` appeared or vanished. Each is cheap
and needs no evaluation. Whether the general form — *diff the structural
assumptions the Portfile makes against the new upstream tree* — survives outside
Go idiom is unproven, and the sample was overwhelmingly Go.

---

## Verification axes

Two axes, not one boolean:

- **Depth** — `--depth=patch|build|artifact|matrix|dependents`
- **Location** — `--where=local|vm|fork-ci|auto` (see `verification.md`: `local` means an ephemeral prefix born clean per run, not the user's `/opt/local` and not a standing test prefix; upstream PR CI is never a `--where` value — it is incurred by `promote`, not chosen)

They are genuinely orthogonal: build-depth verification can run on a laptop or a
runner, and a boolean cannot express "build depth, but in CI because this port is
heavy", which is the motivating case. Default `--where=auto`, inferred from
observed build times rather than asked.

*"CI" is two different things, and only one of them is a location.* An earlier
draft of this section called `--where=ci` a category error. Half right:

- **The buildbot** is not drivable. It runs post-merge, on its own schedule,
  and reports back. It is a findings *source*, never a `--where` value.
- **Fork CI is drivable, and it is the cheapest pristine environment in the
  design.** `macports-ports`' own workflow triggers on `push:
  branches-ignore: master` — a bare branch push to a fork runs the full
  lint-and-build matrix (`macos-14/15/26`), with `mpbb` selecting changed
  ports and a fresh MacPorts bootstrap on every clean runner. No PR needed;
  `cancel-in-progress` makes re-pushes cheap. Verified against a real fork:
  405 runs, all `event: push`, all on feature branches. GitHub Actions is
  free with unlimited minutes on public repositories for every account tier,
  macOS runners included, and a fork of a public repository is public.

So `--where=ci` stands, meaning fork CI, with honest limits stated at plan
time. **Enablement**: Actions are off by default on a fresh fork until the
owner enables them — probe-able via the runs API, a fact about the machine in
D11's discovered tier. **Concurrency**: the free plan allows 5 concurrent
macOS jobs and the matrix costs 3 per push, so fork CI verifies roughly one
branch at a time — a per-change gate, not a sweep engine. **Coverage**: the
standard runners are all Apple Silicon; fork CI buys three macOS versions and
zero Intel. **No preserved environment**: the runner evaporates, so D7's
debug-in-snapshot disposition never attaches to a fork-CI finding.

Three design consequences. *First*, fork CI answers D4's third proposition —
declaration completeness in a pristine environment — for free, on three OS
versions, which is most of what the VM executor existed to provide; the VM's
remaining value narrows to interactivity, pinned environments, and no
concurrency cap. *Second*, the branch dockhand pushes for verification is the
same branch `promote` turns into a PR — verification and publication share an
artifact, and a promoted branch arrives pre-verified. *Third*, `--where` values
are not interchangeable executors with a common shape: local is synchronous and
preserves its environment on failure (the D7 debug handle), fork CI is
asynchronous and returns only findings. D5's single-interface assumption
should be re-examined against that asymmetry — `verification.md` now carries
that re-examination.

*(field)* `artifact` is a new rung, and it may be a new *proposition* rather than
a rung — see the open question below. Four ports compiled, linked, destrooted
and passed `port lint` while the version stamp the update existed to change had
silently not happened. What caught all four was running the built binary and
reading its self-reported version.

---

## `promote` emits commits, not just a branch *(PR evidence)*

The design treated `promote`'s output as "branch, push, PR" with the diff as
the reviewable artifact. Review culture says otherwise: the **commit** is the
unit reviewers police, and commit hygiene is a top source of blocking feedback
(`pr-evidence.md` §2–3). So a plan destined for promotion must carry a **commit
plan**:

- **Grouping.** One logical change per commit. A bump and its dependent
  revbumps are different logical changes sharing a PR: the bump is one commit,
  the grouped revbump of N dependents is one more — observed at N=9.
- **Messages.** `portname: short description` — enforced verbatim in review;
  a wrong format is a blocking verdict by itself. Multi-port commits join
  names: `arm-binutils, m68k-binutils: …`. For a bump, the project's own
  convention (`<subport>: update to <version>`) is already printed by
  `port bump --patch`.
- **Titles.** The PR template *auto-detects* change type from the title
  ("update" requires ": update to" in the title), so titles are load-bearing
  for the project's tooling, not just prose.
- **Trailers.** Trac cross-references go in the commit body in the literal form
  `Closes: https://trac.macports.org/ticket/NNNNN`. Which means `promote`
  wants an optional `--closes <ticket>` and findings should carry ticket URLs
  when they originate from one.

None of this existed in any dockhand document before the PR sweep, and all of
it is mechanical. It is also the cheapest goodwill available: §11 of the PR
evidence finds review labour dominated by convention-completeness, not by
wrong edits — which is precisely the labour a convention-aware tool removes.

---

## The 72-hour window shapes everything after `promote`

From the project's own update policies (guide.macports.org, "Port Update
Policies"), not from observation: **nomaintainer** ports may be updated by
anyone; **openmaintainer** ports allow minor updates by others, but the PR
still waits 72 hours before merge so the maintainer can review; **maintained**
ports require maintainer approval, with a 72-hour timeout after which other
committers may proceed if they document the timeout in the commit message.
Ports one maintains oneself carry no window.

This is a fact no dockhand document accounted for, and it restructures the
tail of the pipeline:

- **The merge actor is not the user.** dockhand's primary persona is a
  maintainer *without* commit access: `promote` opens the PR, but a core-team
  committer merges it. Window expiry therefore enables *a committer*, never
  the user — the policy language about "proceeding after a documented
  timeout" is committer-only. For the dockhand user, the binding constraint
  after `promote` is committer attention, on which the window is only a lower
  bound.
- **Fleet maintenance is a queue of PRs aging through mandatory windows.**
  The latency floor is set by policy, not tooling — no verification speed
  removes it. A sweep does not produce N merges; it produces N windows aging
  in parallel, most of them then waiting further for a committer to notice.
- **PR age against its window is a first-class `status` field.** "41h into
  72" — and past expiry, "window expired 6 days, no committer action" — is
  exactly what a maintainer checking in wants to know, alongside CI state. A
  PR aged well past its window is a finding, and its remedy is a polite
  follow-up (the guide's own channel: a request to macports-dev, or a PR
  comment). Pinging spends attention, so it is ring 3: dockhand may *propose*
  a ping, never send one unattended, and never twice.
- **`promote` reads the `maintainers` field to select the window** — which
  largely answers open question 3 below. The field is a policy selector:
  nomaintainer → proceed; openmaintainer → PR, 72-hour clock; maintained →
  notify and wait, and on timeout the commit message must document it, which
  is a provenance line `promote` can write correctly every time (humans
  forget it).
- **D9's autonomy gains a time dimension it already implied.** Auto-PR was
  never auto-merge — and for the non-committer persona, even wholly-owned
  ports terminate in a committer's merge. Every change dockhand produces ends
  in someone else's attention; the core team's review capacity is the global
  bottleneck. That makes convention-completeness the throughput lever, not a
  courtesy: each review round-trip costs days at PR latency, and the PR
  evidence shows round-trips are dominated by exactly the conventions the
  commit plan automates. The fastest PR is the one a committer can merge
  without typing anything.

---

## Exit codes

Refusal is a feature, so a decline must be distinguishable from a failure:
`0` plan produced, `1` error, `2` declined.

The point/sweep asymmetry lands here directly. A point intent that declines
exits `2` — the user asked for one thing and did not get it. A sweep that
declines on 40 of 340 ports exits `0`, because that is a success with a tail. If
both exit alike, every CI wrapper around a sweep is wrong.

---

## What is deliberately absent

No `dockhand init`, no `dockhand db`, no `dockhand sync`. D8 holds that
everything persisted is re-derivable from the ports tree plus GitHub, so there
is no store to initialise, migrate or reconcile — and the moment one of those
commands looks necessary, D8 has been violated. The empty space in the verb list
is the invariant made visible.

---

## Open questions

1. **Is `artifact` a rung or a fourth proposition?** D4 names three independent
   propositions and the field evidence produced a case none of them span: the
   edit was faithful, the build succeeded, dependencies were declared, and the
   result was still wrong. If it is a fourth proposition it does not belong on
   the `--depth` ladder at all, since that ladder is ordered by cost and this is
   not more expensive than a build — it is a different question asked of the
   same artifact. It is also only *sometimes available*, which none of the
   existing three are.

2. ~~**How does a cohort reach GitHub?**~~ *Answered by PR evidence.* As one
   PR: multi-port PRs are routine when the ports move for one reason — a
   nine-port revbump across four maintainers merged without objection, and one
   review *required* the cross-port pairing in a single PR. "One PR per port"
   was wrong; the norm is one PR per logical change, and the unit reviewers
   police is the commit (`pr-evidence.md` §1–2). What replaces this question is
   the commit plan — see **`promote` emits commits, not just a branch**.

3. ~~**Does `promote` read the `maintainers` field as permission?**~~
   *Largely answered by the project's published update policies — see "The
   72-hour window" above.* The field selects the policy: nomaintainer
   (proceed), openmaintainer (minor changes via PR plus the 72-hour window),
   maintained (notify, wait, document any timeout in the commit message).
   What remains open is only the edge: what counts as a "minor" update on an
   openmaintainer port is judgement, and dockhand should classify
   conservatively — tier alone does not decide it.

4. **Can `promote` fill the PR template truthfully?** The template asks for
   `Tested on` and a verification checklist. A tool knows exactly which rungs
   ran, which is precisely what D9's provenance requirement wants — and it is a
   better answer than a human ticking "tried a full install" when only
   `destroot` ran. *PR evidence says yes and lowers the bar:* "Tested on: CI
   only." was accepted without comment in a merged nine-port PR, and a
   maintainer's bot PR with a fully-filled template merged without friction
   (`pr-evidence.md` §8–9). Candour is the accepted currency; unverified
   assertions are what draw "did you verify this?".
